From: Ferestre contributors
Subject: Hold the package header to the package service's hash

The package service is reached over TLS with the account's token, and the
CDN roots it lists are plain http. A streaming install checks every page
against the package's hash tree and the tree against the top hash in the
header, but nothing held the header itself, so a download rewritten in
transit -- header, tree and pages together -- was installed.

When the package service gives a HashOfHashes for the package, the header's
top hash (top_hash_block_hash, the SHA-256 of the top page of the hash tree)
must equal it. It is compared as soon as the header is read, before the tree
it names is, and a header that does not match is refused with an error that
says so; nothing is installed. A HashOfHashes that is not a SHA-256 in hex or
base64 is refused as well. XvdFile::parse_anchored does the check; the
streaming install uses it when the package came from the package service.
Reading HashOfHashes as the top hash is an interpretation: no package seen
so far carries one, so it is unconfirmed, and a package that carries one
meaning something else fails with that error rather than installing.

When the service gives none -- every package seen so far, Stardew Valley's
among them, has no HashOfHashes and an empty FileHash for each file -- the
install goes ahead as before and says on stderr that the header is checked
against nothing outside the download. The per-file FileHash is not used:
nothing in the header corresponds to it, and a streaming install never reads
a whole file. XODUS_REQUIRE_HTTPS_CDN=1 still refuses plain http roots.

